 the very best at that time i mean  mike really i mean right i mean if we're  gonna make a comparison to this day and  age now i mean he's the tom brady of  that era right i mean i think that's the  closest comparison vince lombardi bill  belichick that's kind of synonymous bart  star tom brady yeah guys that were  clutch able to lead teams you know take  the coaching of a tough you know uh kind  of tyrant type of head coach that way  where it's just i'm in charge and this  is the way it is and we do things my way  but the ultimate winner nonetheless and  hey i do feel like sometimes some of  these all-time greats and i know we said  this a little over text you and i  yesterday but they kind of at times i  feel like do get shuffled under the rug  a little bit as far as we don't take  notoriety as them being the greatest  quarterbacks of all time bart starr was  the greatest quarterback of the 60s he  did special things i feel like as time  goes on we do forget these names he's up  there with the johnny unitas of the  world and the sonny jurgensens and the  joe namaths and  you know as time goes on i think we talk  less and less about them  and think about how the rules have  changed and it started in the 70s to  protect quarterbacks to make it easier  to have a passing game used to be you  could mug the receiver all the way down  the field until the ball was in the end  that's right that that one chuck in the  first five yards was a creature of the  mid 70s in an effort to make the game  more offensively productive and thus  exciting and and star thrived as a  passer especially in postseason settings  before those rule changes were made  think about this yeah in the 1966 post  season right star's passer rating was  143.5 wow for the regular season the  average passer rating was 67.4 yeah that  that's it's unbelievable it really is i  mean hey he's still uh and correct me if  i'm wrong stats or anybody but i mean  still the only quarterback to ever win  three straight championships right he  won an nfl championship then won super  bowl one and super bowl two to follow it  up uh to kind of put the nfl you know on  the map as the king over the afl at the  time you ever watched the old things  about the packers and you know vince  lombardi the pressure he was under uh  from the nfl to win those first two  matchups to show that they were the  greater league over the afl but you know  truly uh a great story bart starr  alabama he's really the one that kind of  started the quarterback factory from  alabama right to where you know the ken  stablers and the joe namas and everybody  else that came after it were a product  of what bart starr did there to begin  with  career-high single season passing  yardage for bart starr 2438  as a difference  it was a big deal if you had 200 yards  in a game back in those days  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports

